I just put that to ensure that it wasn't implicitly using c++0x when -stdlib=libc++. I think the c++03 standard is technically better in that it fixes problems with the 98 standard. The thing is that g++ defaults not to -std=c++98 but -std=g++98, which enables some g++ extensions that might not be portable, particularly to MSVC (which I guess we haven't messed with in several months).

