On Sunday, December 16, 2012 2:54:33 PM UTC-5, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> I don't know if this warning is benign or not.  This
> is partly why I'm worried about the fact that the
> C++11 standard changed the internal type returns inside
> the base iterator type we're using:
>
>         std::istreambuf_iterator<char>
>
> Here's the link again, indicating changed behavior as of g++ 4.7:
>
> http://gcc.gnu.org/bugzilla/show_bug.cgi?id=50336
>
> http://gcc.1065356.n5.nabble.com/Bug-libstdc-50336-New-LWG-issue-445-td347525.html

Maybe, but I would think that since the c++98 standard involved a char& and the c++11 standard involves a char, then if somehow some code were expecting a char& and gets a char, then it should be a compiler error.

But, since we can get the parser errors without these warnings if Boost 1.50.0 is used and can get warnings without errors with the Spirit example under Boost 1.52.0, then we must have a weakness in the grammar rules, and Boost 1.52.0 has a separate weakness.

Ben

