> somehow some code were expecting a char& and gets a char, then it should be a compiler error.

It's C++!

Ah, but if it's in a return type, you get reference-of
applied automatically.   And the reverse is just fine,
assigning a char& to a char.

> But, since we can get the parser errors without these warnings if Boost 1.50.0 is used and can get warnings without
> errors with the Spirit example under Boost 1.52.0, then we must have a weakness in the grammar rules, and Boost 1.52.0
> has a separate weakness.

I think that's the right diagnosis.  I'd be more comfortable if
we weren't using the istreambuf_iterator, but I tried using
an istream_iterator and get weird behavior even in situations where
istreambuf_iterator works.

I want to sit down with Daniel and do a thorough code
review of the parser.  I'm hoping it's something stupid I'm
just not seeing.

Another avenue would be to just read whatever input we get
into a string and then use a string iterator or something.
I'm not that familiar with all the iterator classes, but I'm
thinking it should be possible.

If that doesn't work, the next thing I want to do is try to
create as simple a standalone example as possible that causes
the error.  Then we can try the Boost list again, even though it
hasn't panned out in the past.

- Bob

