Subject: llvm-g++ and the sign() function
Message-ID: <622l3np6KBU.PV5ZEVgDdsUJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-dev/c/622l3np6KBU/m/PV5ZEVgDdsUJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The compiler errors I have hit with double_exponential under llvm-g++ are not our fault, since I can reproduce the problem just by trying to compile

goodrich@CYBERPOWERPC:/tmp$ cat sign.cpp
#include <boost/math/special_functions/sign.hpp>
#include <iostream>
int main() {
  std::cout << "expect 1, get " << boost::math::sign(10.0) << std::endl;
  std::cout << "expect 0, get " << boost::math::sign(0.0) << std::endl;
  std::cout << "expect -1, get " << boost::math::sign(-5.0) << std::endl;
  return 0;
}

but we can work around it by defining our own sign() function instead of using boost's, e.g. this patch

diff --git a/src/stan/prob/distributions/univariate/continuous/double_exponential.hpp b/src/stan/prob/distributions/univariate/continuous/double_exponential.hpp
index ccea83a..f2c14d2 100644
--- a/src/stan/prob/distributions/univariate/continuous/double_exponential.hpp
+++ b/src/stan/prob/distributions/univariate/continuous/double_exponential.hpp
@@ -7,11 +7,15 @@
 #include <stan/meta/traits.hpp>
 #include <stan/prob/constants.hpp>
 #include <stan/prob/traits.hpp>
-#include <boost/math/special_functions/sign.hpp>
+//#include <boost/math/special_functions/sign.hpp>

 namespace stan {

   namespace prob {
+    template<typename T>
+    inline int sign(T& z) {
+      return (z == 0) ? 0 : z < 0 ? -1 : 1;
+    }

     // DoubleExponential(y|mu,sigma)  [sigma > 0]
     // FIXME: add documentation
@@ -32,7 +36,7 @@ namespace stan {
       using stan::prob::include_summand;
       using std::log;
       using std::fabs;
-      using boost::math::sign;
+ //     using boost::math::sign;

I have not figured out why boost's implementation of the sign function

http://www.boost.org/doc/libs/1_52_0/boost/math/special_functions/sign.hpp

is so complicated. I'm sure there is a reason, but I doubt that reason is relevant to Stan.

With that patch I can get llvm-g++ to pass test-unit and so far it has not broken any other configuration I have tried. I doubt many people would really want to use llvm-g++, but it does get us closer to being able to do on Linux what Xcode does on the Mac, except Xcode is using 4.2.1 and I am using 4.6. Does anyone see a compelling reason to stick with boost's sign function?

Ben

